  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 364,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 164,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54217,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 164,
        "headingText": "<p>FGPs arise exclusively in the oxyntic mucosa.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:18:39"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 365,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 165,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54218,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 165,
        "headingText": "<p>These polyps are usually asymptomatic, discovered as incidental findings on endoscopy {19491866; 23583466; 25458775}. When FGPs are found in young patients (especially when the polyps are numerous, e.g. &ge;&nbsp;20), a polyposis syndrome must be considered. When FGPs are associated with duodenal adenomas, a familial polyposis syndrome should be strongly suspected, and colonoscopy is recommended {23583466}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:18:51"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 366,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 166,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54219,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 166,
        "headingText": "<p>These are the most common type of polyps detected at oesophagogastroduodenoscopy. They may occur at any age and have a distinct female predominance {19465154}. In a large pathology study in the USA, FGPs were diagnosed in 7.2% of patients who had an oesophagogastroduodenoscopy and constituted 70&ndash;90% of all gastric polyps submitted for histopathological evaluation {25458775}. Individuals with familial polyposis syndromes are typically younger than the average patient with FGPs (mean age: 40&nbsp;years) {23583466}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:19:03"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 367,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 167,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54220,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 167,
        "headingText": "<p>They may occur sporadically or as part of several polyposis syndromes, including familial adenomatous polyposis and gastric adenocarcinoma and proximal polyposis of the stomach (GAPPS) {18237868}. When first discovered, these polyps were believed to be hamartomatous; however, their association with proton-pump inhibitor use, confirmed in several studies {27211501; 27634363}, suggests that mechanisms in the sporadic setting can be related to the suppression of acid secretion by proton-pump inhibition as part of their pathogenesis. FGPs are inversely related to <em>Helicobacter pylori</em> gastritis: &lt;&nbsp;1 in 200&nbsp;patients with <em>H.&nbsp;pylori</em> gastritis have FGPs {19491866; 23583466}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:19:14"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 368,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 168,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54221,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 168,
        "headingText": "<p>The polyps result from hyperplasia and dilatation of oxyntic glands. Sporadic FGPs are devoid of <em>APC</em> mutations but can have mutations in <em>CTNNB1</em> (encoding &beta;-catenin). Epigenetic alterations involving methylation of CpG islands play a role in the development of some FGPs with a proton-pump inhibitor effect {15491970}. <em>CTNNB1</em> mutation is seen in 64&ndash;91% of sporadic FGPs without dysplasia {11238048; 11956818}. Dysplastic FGPs may harbour <em>APC</em> mutations, typically together with wildtype <em>CTNNB1</em> {12414520}. Familial adenomatous polyposis&ndash;associated FGPs feature biallelic <em>APC</em> mutations and wildtype <em>CTNNB1</em> {11238048}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:19:26"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 375,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 207,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54222,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 207,
        "headingText": "<p>FGPs occur as multiple small polyps (typically &lt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;mm).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:19:36"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 369,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 169,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54223,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 169,
        "headingText": "<p>FGPs typically show dilated oxyntic glands, foveolar hypoplasia, and parietal hyperplasia. Polyp erosion can coexist with regenerative changes that may be misinterpreted as dysplasia. Dysplasia is rare in sporadic FGPs, but it is not uncommon in the syndromic setting (see <a style=\"cursor:pointer\" href=\"/chaptercontent/31/229\" target=\"_blank\">section </a><em>GAPPS and other fundic gland polyposes</em>).</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-06-04T15:18:23"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 370,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 170,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 54224,
        "bookId": 31,
        "chapterId": 20,
        "headingId": 170,
        "headingText": "<p>Not clinically relevant</p>",
        "timestamp": "2019-01-29T15:19:59"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 371,
    "bookid": 31,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 171,
    "contributions": [
      {
